digitization: drain the site-worker's tanks here every 10 minutes

Every form on the site sends its reply to the site-worker, which only keeps it in a KV tank. This adds the
crew line that brings them here, so the replies live on our own machine and the log (Microsoft 365, by any
method) can be fed from here at leisure. No worker code changes: the line runs the site-worker's own
`bin/drain.mjs` (site-worker #7).

- `machines/digitization/drain`: `keys run cloudflare -- /usr/bin/node <site-worker>/bin/drain.mjs`.
  Each reply becomes `~/fcpm/answers/<tank>/<received>--<reference>.json` on the card's encrypted home,
  700/600. It is written, read back and compared before its KV key is deleted, so a reply is always in
  one place or both. A failure leaves it in KV for the next pass. Passes `--keep` and `--tank` through.
- `services`: `drain every 10m`. That is 144 list operations a day, far under KV's 1,000-a-day free
  limit.
- `follow`: keeps `~/code/work/site-worker` (beside this checkout, its own repository, not a submodule) on
  the origin's main, fast-forward only, never over local changes. It clones it if it is missing.
- `check.sh`: the site-worker checkout is there, and the archive is private.
- `keys.yml`: the `cloudflare` group lists the drain as a user. `PROFILE.md` gets a Replies section.

Debian's `/usr/bin/node` (v20, from gear `npm`) runs it: the crew's service has no person's PATH.

Merge qualifications:
- [x] `drain --keep` from this branch's script: 1 reply found and saved, nothing deleted (written to a
      scratch archive, then removed)
- [x] `crew.py` reads the new line with nothing reported; its start line resolves
- [x] cloning `FC-Public-Media/site-worker` from the origin as this box's user works
- [x] YAML parses; shell syntax checks pass

After merge: `follow` puts it in place within 5 minutes. `work/site-worker` already exists, so it is
re-pointed at the origin and fast-forwarded, and the crew starts the line when it sees the new
`services`. The first real pass empties the one reply waiting now (in `program`).

Carried, not decided:
- The `cloudflare` group is the org account's deploy token. The drain only needs KV read and delete, so a
  narrower token in a group of its own would be safer. Not made yet.
- Carrying the archive on to the M365 log is the next line. It reads these files; KV is not asked again.

